","To find the equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \), we use the slope of the tangent line at that point, which is the derivative of the function at \( a \), denoted by \( f'(a) \).

The equation of a line with slope \( m \) passing through the point \( (a, b) \) is given by the equation:
\[ y = m(x - a) + b \]

In this case, the slope of the tangent line is \( f'(a) \), and the point through which the line passes is \( (a, f(a)) \). Substituting these values into the equation of a line, we get:
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a) \]

This is the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).

Thus, the equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is:
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a) \]

