","To determine the distance from the start where b catches up with a, we first calculate the distance a has traveled before b starts cycling.
a walks at 10 kmph, and b starts 3 hours after a starts.
Distance a travels in 3 hours = speed × time = 10 kmph × 3 hours = 30 km.
When b starts cycling, a is already 30 km away from the start.
Now, b cycles at 20 kmh, and a continues to walk at 10 kmh.
The relative speed of b with respect to a is the difference in their speeds, since b is moving in the same direction as a.
Relative speed = 20 kmph - 10 kmph = 10 kmph.
The time taken for b to catch up with a is the distance a is away from the start when b starts, divided by the relative speed.
Time taken for b to catch up with a = distance / relative speed = 30 km / 10 kmph = 3 hours.
Since b cycles for 3 hours to catch up with a, the distance b travels is:
Distance b travels = speed × time = 20 kmph × 3 hours = 60 km.
Since b starts 3 hours after a, the time a takes to reach the point where b catches up with him is:
Total time for a = 3 hours (initial) + 3 hours = 6 hours.
Distance a travels = speed × time = 10 kmph × 6 hours = 60 km.
Since b and a are at the same position when b catches up with a, the distance from the start is the same for both.
The distance from the start is 60 km.

Final answer: $\boxed{60 \text{ km}}$",fluency,"# Task Description
